Move the subscription `reset()` ahead of the command whose report it awaits.

Upstream `TC_GC_2_3`, `TC_GC_2_4` and `TC_GC_2_5` send a Groupcast command
that mutates `Membership`, and only then call `<sub>.reset()` before awaiting
the report of that very mutation. `reset()` clears the accumulated reports and
flushes the queue, so a device that reports promptly loses: the report lands in
the few milliseconds between the command response and the `reset()`, is
discarded, and the subsequent `await_all_expected_report_matches` waits the
full timeout for a report that can never come (the attribute does not change
again, and max-interval reports are empty liveness heartbeats).

These subscriptions use `min_interval = 0`, so reporting immediately is both
spec-correct and desirable; rs-matter does exactly that and wins or loses the
race depending on host load. `TC_GC_2_8` already shows the correct shape -
`reset()` first, then the command - and this patch applies it to the other
three. No test semantics change: the reset still discards everything observed
before the mutation under test.

Reported upstream; drop this patch once the fix lands and the CHIP pin moves
past it. See `CHIP_PATCHES` in `xtask/src/itest/common.rs`.

